# Commit Conventions

This project uses [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) for clarity in review and history. If you wire in a release automation later (Changesets, release-please, semantic-release), it will read these prefixes — but the convention is useful on its own even without one.

## Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <short description>

<optional body — explain WHY, not WHAT>

<optional footer — BREAKING CHANGE: ..., refs: ...>
```

## Types

| Type | Use for |
|---|---|
| `feat` | new user-facing feature |
| `fix` | bug fix |
| `feat!` / `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer | breaking change |
| `docs` | documentation only |
| `style` | whitespace, formatting (no behavior change) |
| `refactor` | code change without behavior change |
| `perf` | performance fix |
| `test` | test additions / changes |
| `build` | build system, deps |
| `ci` | CI / workflow / pipeline changes |
| `chore` | tooling, internal cleanup |
| `revert` | reverting a prior commit |

If you adopt a release automation that reads commit types (e.g. release-please), `feat:` → minor, `fix:`/`perf:` → patch, `feat!:` → major. Otherwise these are descriptive only.

## Scope

Optional. Use the area of the change. Common scopes for ShipAPI apps:

- `(api)` / `(routes)` / `(modules)` — module surface changes
- `(db)` — schema, migrations, queries
- `(auth)` — authentication or permissions
- `(jobs)` — BullMQ jobs / cron
- `(test)` — when type is `chore` / `refactor` and only tests change

Skip the scope when it's not informative — a one-word commit needs no scope.

## Examples

```
feat(api): add POST /posts/:id/publish

Endpoint already gates on posts:publish permission and emits a
post.published outbox event for the notifications worker.

---

fix(auth): refresh token rotation no longer races on logout

The refresh table delete and new-token insert ran in separate
transactions; concurrent logout + refresh could double-spend a
token. Wrap both in withTransaction.

---

ci: skip e2e suite on docs-only PRs

paths-ignore was matching .md files but not the docs/ tree.

---

feat!(api): drop /v1/* routes; clients must move to /v2/*

BREAKING CHANGE: Endpoints under /v1/ have been removed. See
docs/MIGRATION_V1_TO_V2.md for the field-by-field map.
```

## Don't

- Don't use `git commit --amend` after pushing — it rewrites history and breaks anyone who pulled.
- Don't `--no-verify`. If a hook fails, fix the underlying issue. Bypassing hooks is how secrets get committed.
- Don't write multi-paragraph commit bodies for trivial changes. The body should add information the diff doesn't.
- Don't reference internal ticket IDs in the title — the body is fine, but titles get rendered in CHANGELOG.
